**Read:** [Applied Mechanics of Solids — Allan F. Bower](../../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Applied-Mechanics-of-Solids-Bower.lingua#r_109WFZBWMEMGHV9TGFD22H2F1W), sections 9.2 and 9.6; [Materials structure and observation — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Materials-Structure-and-Observation-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_57315283EDNWKYC1PQE23G2AKF), Fibre Composites' failure and toughness sections; [Wood Handbook — Forest Products Laboratory, 2021](../sources/Wood-Handbook-FPL-2021.lingua#r_2SP2KMHCD78MQGZN69GKPGJ3WM), chapters 10–12 for bonded wood products.

Fibre tension, fibre kinking, matrix cracking, debonding and delamination are different mechanisms. A scalar interaction criterion may indicate a fitted onset envelope without identifying which mechanism occurs or how stiffness changes afterward. First-ply failure does not necessarily equal total laminate failure.

Interface fracture depends on mode mix, geometry, residual stress and the chosen resistance definition. Manufacturing defects and moisture can change both constituent and interface behavior. For a sandwich, face damage, core crushing and face/core debonding need separate load paths.

**Work:** Compare a maximum-stress and interaction-criterion prediction for a declared lamina dataset, then check mechanism-specific evidence. Trace a plausible progression from matrix crack to delamination without inventing a universal stiffness-knockdown rule. Explain how inspection sensitivity and specimen preparation affect the evidence for an initially undamaged state.

